{
  "dom_id": "step-q334297",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247879/assets/ffedd1b83fa2d80b924b0d2f68a3100236347e3222ec171bc60b299579a7af4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$",
